{"files":[{"content":"#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n#include <math.h>\n\n#define N_ITER 10 // number of simulation iterations\n#define DT 0.01f // time step\n#define SOFTENING 1e-9f // to avoid zero divisors\n\n/*\n * Each body holds coordinate positions (i.e., x, y, and z) and\n * velocities (i.e., vx, vy, and vz).\n */\ntypedef struct { float x, y, z, vx, vy, vz; } Body;\n\n/*\n * Compute the gravitational impact on one target body.\n */\nstatic void body_force_one(Body *p, float dt, int n, int i) {\n  float fx = 0.0f;\n  float fy = 0.0f;\n  float fz = 0.0f;\n\n  for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n    float dx = p[j].x - p[i].x;\n    float dy = p[j].y - p[i].y;\n    float dz = p[j].z - p[i].z;\n    float sqrd_dist = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz + SOFTENING;\n    float inv_dist = 1 / sqrt(sqrd_dist);\n    float inv_dist3 = inv_dist * inv_dist * inv_dist;\n\n    fx += dx * inv_dist3;\n    fy += dy * inv_dist3;\n    fz += dz * inv_dist3;\n  }\n\n  p[i].vx += dt*fx;\n  p[i].vy += dt*fy;\n  p[i].vz += dt*fz;\n}\n\n/*\n * Read a binary dataset with initialized bodies.\n */\nBody* read_dataset(int *nbodies) {\n  int b = fread(nbodies, sizeof(*nbodies), 1, stdin);\n  if (b != 1) {\n    fprintf(stderr, \"\\nError reading nbodie value\\n\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n  }\n  Body *bodies = (Body *)malloc(*nbodies * sizeof(Body));\n  b = fread(bodies, *nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdin);\n  if (b != 1) {\n    fprintf(stderr, \"\\nError reading input values\\n\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n  }\n\n  return bodies;\n}\n\n/*\n * Write simulation results into a binary dataset.\n */\nvoid write_dataset(const int nbodies, Body *bodies) {\n  int b = fwrite(bodies, nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdout);\n  if (b != 1) {\n    fprintf(stderr, \"\\nError writing to output\\n\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n  }\n}\n\nint main(int argc, char **argv) {\n  int nbodies;\n  Body *bodies = read_dataset(&nbodies);\n\n  /* Reuse one team across iterations; static scheduling suits uniform work. */\n  #pragma omp parallel if (nbodies >= 64)\n  {\n    for (int iter = 0; iter < N_ITER; iter++) {\n      #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n      for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; i++) {\n        body_force_one(bodies, DT, nbodies, i);\n      }\n\n      /* The barrier above is required before positions are integrated. */\n      #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n      for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; i++) {\n        bodies[i].x += bodies[i].vx * DT;\n        bodies[i].y += bodies[i].vy * DT;\n        bodies[i].z += bodies[i].vz * DT;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  write_dataset(nbodies, bodies);\n  free(bodies);\n  return EXIT_SUCCESS;\n}\n","path":"src/nbody.c"}],"hypothesis":"A persistent OpenMP team with static target-body work sharing should reduce team-creation and scheduling overhead while using the four available cores for sufficiently large inputs. A serial threshold avoids parallel overhead on small inputs.","notes":"Force accumulation remains in ascending source-body order for each target. The force and integration work-sharing loops retain their implicit barriers to preserve phase ordering and avoid position read/write races.","research":{"queries":[],"urls":[]},"sources_kept":[],"transformations":["Reuse one OpenMP parallel region across all simulation iterations instead of creating a team for each loop.","Use schedule(static) for the uniform-cost target-body and integration loops.","Use a serial OpenMP region for inputs with fewer than 64 bodies to limit thread overhead." ]}